    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9aa7fdbe_7ad31b04",
        "filename": "keystone/assignment/controllers.py",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 966,
      "author": {
        "id": 9142
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-10-23T19:46:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Suppose the following use case:\n\nAs a project admin, I\u0027d like to know what role assignments are being applied to my project (filter by project) for a given group  of users (suppose master_devs :-) inside my domain (filter by group).\n\nDoes it make sense?",
      "parentUuid": "9aa7fdbe_6c59e0fd",
      "revId": "cad67e293005f73713e20a42b81b7112294d2bd6",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9aa7fdbe_6b6fcf2b",
        "filename": "keystone/assignment/controllers.py",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 966,
      "author": {
        "id": 5707
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-10-23T22:19:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So it that makes sense in non-effective mode, but I don\u0027t think it is does if you have specified effective. Two reasons:\n\n1) I\u0027m not sure it makes sense it terms of the REST API - at least as it is specified today. Specifying a filter is meant to be a function applied to the \"output of the API\"...i.e. you should only be able to filter on attributes that are being returned (and groups are never returned as part of an effective list_role_assignments call)\n\n2) It also doesn\u0027t make algorithmic sense...imagine (in your example) if a given role is assigned to a project in more than one way (e.g. a direct role AND via a group role)....would you include it or not?  In non-effective mode it\u0027s obvious what to do - since you would be asking explicitly about group assignments...but in effective-mode we are meant to normalise both groups and inheritance...so do you consider the assignment to have come from the group assignment or the direct one?",
      "parentUuid": "9aa7fdbe_7ad31b04",
      "revId": "cad67e293005f73713e20a42b81b7112294d2bd6",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9aa7fdbe_e4d924f4",
        "filename": "keystone/assignment/controllers.py",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 966,
      "author": {
        "id": 9142
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-10-24T12:07:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "1) Ok ..\n2) We could include both since we have information from where it comes from inside the \u0027links\u0027 entry on the dict.\nTake a look at _build_project_equivalent_of_group_domain_role(..), we have group information on [\u0027links\u0027][\u0027assignment\u0027] and on [\u0027links\u0027][\u0027membership\u0027].\nSo we\u0027d be able to distinguish when it comes directly and when it comes via a group role.\n\nHowever, I think it is not related to this patch, then I will make the change to avoid the use of \u0027effective\u0027 and specifying a \u0027group_id\u0027 together, ok? I\u0027ll add a verification on the list_role_assignments method, as we do for constraints on combining filters.",
      "parentUuid": "9aa7fdbe_6b6fcf2b",
      "revId": "cad67e293005f73713e20a42b81b7112294d2bd6",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9aa7fdbe_f57aac22",
        "filename": "keystone/assignment/controllers.py",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 966,
      "author": {
        "id": 5707
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-10-24T13:47:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "...so I think you are correct on 2)...we *could* distinguish (I knew there was a reason I put that membership info in there!!!)....but I think for now, we should not allow the group filter in effective mode.",
      "parentUuid": "9aa7fdbe_e4d924f4",
      "revId": "cad67e293005f73713e20a42b81b7112294d2bd6",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
